t that alcohol  evaporates and even if you're quite  experienced at formulating you can still  make measurement errors in a properly  formulated product they will re measure  the alcohol concentration at the end to  make sure whatever you put in is what  you actually get out pharmaceutical and  cosmetic grade ingredients are generally  safe to repeatedly put on your hands  food grade is also generally safe  anything else is a bit of a crapshoot  but the only recipes that I endorse  the ones that the World Health  Organization has put out and even then  only if it's made by someone who knows  what they're doing can I add this  particular ingredient to my hand  sanitizer when you're making a recipe  that has other ingredients apart from  what the World Health Organization has  recommended you are running into a bunch  of risks these ingredients are water  alcohol glycerin or glycerol and  hydrogen peroxide a lot of people have  asked me if they can add oils to their  hand sanitizers so if you're using  something oil-based essential oils or  vitamin E oil these oils do not mix with  water in a properly formulated product  you have to add soluble Isis to make  sure the oil actually disperses and  doesn't just sit on top and so as  possible you'll squirt out a handful  that is mostly oil and only a tiny bit  of alcohol and then even though overall  the whole thing has a safe amount of  alcohol a particular amount that you  dispense may not I've also been asked  about adding moisturizing ingredients to  protect your hands from drying out the  World Health Organization recipes use  glycerin as the moisturizer if you use  other moisturizing ingredients you can  run into problems so for example aloe  vera gel is biological matter that can  go off inside your hand sanitiser aloe  vera gel products do often contain  preservatives but once it's mixed into a  hand sanitizer it might not be preserved  enough even with a really high alcohol  content there's also been a lot of  people saying that aloe vera gel balled  up inside their recipe and they don't  know what to do even if you don't see a  large amount balled up in your recipe  you can have small amounts balled up  that you don't quite notice and so if  you get a lump of aloe vera gel again  you're going to be dispensing something  that is less than the required amount of  alcohol onto your hand even if the  overall product has enough alcohol if  you've added some random moisturizer  into your hand sanitizer you might have  any combination of these problems and I  feel like this goes without saying but  don't try to sell your DIY recipe the  regulations around the world generally  treat hand sanitizer as a therapeutic  good and so it's regulated differently  from normal cosmetics where is the link  to the alcohol calculator it's in the  description of that video and of this  video if you expand the description  scroll down a bit  it is  how do you use the alcohol calculator so  once you've clicked the link a new tab  or window will open you might need to  sign into your Google account then click  make a copy you'll have a spreadsheet  look down the bottom for the one called  alcohol calculator click the down arrow  next to the name and click duplicate  this makes a copy so you don't mess up  the original now you can enter your  recipe for example this recipe uses 96%  ethanol it uses 1/3 of the cup of  ethanol you'll need to enter fractions  as decimals the conversions are down  here so 1/3 is not 0.33 change the unit  to cups by pressing the down arrow here  and select the right one then you can  enter the other ingredients the same way  turn any rows you're not using 2-0 then  the percentage of alcohol will show up  here  will ingredients other than alcohol  sanitize my hands I said this in the  last video and it is still true now no  other ingredient apart from a high  alcohol concentration is recommended for  hand sanitizers is alcohol-based hand  sanitizer pedal or surf in water so the  main advantages of hand sanitizer are  it's more portable so you can use it on  the go and it tends to be less drying on  skin than washing your hands apart from  that washing your hands with certain  water is better apart from just killing  the viruses it also washes them away  what is the difference between ethyl  alcohol and isopropyl alcohol they are  different ingredients ethanol or ethyl  alcohol has two carbons isopropanol or  isopropyl alcohol has three they are  very similar but they don't have exactly  the same properties ethanol is gentle on  skins and isopropanol in lots of studies  it has been found to be less irritating  isopropyl alcohol is bigger and so you  generally need a slightly higher amount  to have the same effect so it's  recommended that how much ethanol you  put in is somewhere above 60 to 70  percent isopropyl alcohol is above 70  percent what do you do if your source of  apple has just high enough concentration  to work I recommend using it as the  sanitizer and then moisturizing your  hands afterwards just remember to make  sure that you let the sanitizers stay in  contact with your skin for 20 seconds  before you moisturize what do you do if  your alcohol is lower than 60%  concentration there isn't really much  you can do you can't mix it with  anything to make it a higher  concentration theoretically you could  distill it but I don't recommend doing  this if you don't have the necessary  skills and equipment to do it properly  it is flammable and setting yourself on  fire is much worse than not having hand  sanitizer just wash your hands can you  use methylated spirits methylated  spirits is generally ethanol combined  with something else depending on the  brand it can contain methanol and if it  does I do not recommend using that on  your skin if you don't know what the  other ingredients are I recommend asking  the manufacturer and the warnings about  different grades of products cosmetics I  am acidic or grape all of those 